theory.intro:
A point in space needs three numbers, written as the ordered triple $(x,y,z)$. The $x$-, $y$- and $z$-axes are at right angles to each other and meet at the origin. To reach a point, go along $x$, then across $y$, then up $z$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(-2.4,-2.4) node[below left,font=\scriptsize]{$x$};
\draw[->] (0,0)--(5.8,0) node[right,font=\scriptsize]{$y$};
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,3.6) node[above,font=\scriptsize]{$z$};
\node[above left,font=\scriptsize] at (0,0) {$O$};
\draw[dashed,gray] (0,0)--(-1.5,-1.5) node[midway,right,font=\footnotesize]{$x$};
\draw[dashed,gray] (-1.5,-1.5)--(2.5,-1.5) node[midway,below,font=\footnotesize]{$y$};
\draw[dashed,gray] (2.5,-1.5)--(2.5,0.5) node[midway,right,font=\footnotesize]{$z$};
\fill (2.5,0.5) circle (2.2pt);
\node[above right,font=\footnotesize] at (2.5,0.5) {$P(x,\,y,\,z)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

